  "result": "At dawn, the city belongs to small sounds: a bakery shutter rattling upward, a bicycle chain clicking, pigeons arguing on a rooftop ledge. Before traffic fills the avenues and offices brighten their screens, the streets hold a brief, unclaimed quiet.\n\nI like this hour because it makes ordinary things seem newly invented. A puddle becomes a piece of sky, reflecting clouds that have not yet decided what weather to bring. A bus driver raises one hand to a waiting passenger, and the gesture feels almost ceremonial. A dog trots past with determined purpose, as if it has an appointment with the sunrise.\n\nWe often imagine that meaningful moments must be dramatic: a departure, a discovery, a declaration. Yet much of life is made from smaller arrivals. The warm cup placed beside a bed. The message sent despite uncertainty. The familiar song that unexpectedly returns us to ourselves.\n\nPerhaps attention is a quiet form of gratitude. When we notice the baker\u2019s flour-dusted sleeves, the rainwater trembling on a leaf, or the tired smile exchanged between strangers, we are not merely observing the world. We are participating in it, granting its countless details a little more existence.\n\nBy noon, this magic will be harder to find. The streets will grow louder, and our thoughts will become crowded with tasks. Still, the morning will remain somewhere inside us, a small reserve of stillness. We can return to it simply by looking closely at whatever is near us right now.",
